WEBVTT

NOTE
Journey: Advocates - Week 30 Leader Video
Unit 8, Lesson 2: Questions

1
00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:09.420
So last week we talked about the need for love when we're engaging in conversation

2
00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:12.060
and we want to get out, we want to be loving toward other people.

3
00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:18.220
But if you or any of your students are all like me, that very

4
00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:20.360
concept is terrifying.

5
00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:25.260
The fact that I have to go out, not the love part, that's not so terrifying, but the

6
00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:28.440
part where you go out and actually have a conversation is terrifying.

7
00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:31.120
I need a plan of attack.

8
00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:37.460
Some of your students, I'm sure, are very introverted, very reserved,

9
00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:43.100
and I'm not all that good at the small talk of just sort of having a conversation.

10
00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:47.460
In fact, I actively try to avoid small talk.

11
00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:52.900
I like to get to the point. So when I'm entering a conversation about spiritual

12
00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:57.100
things or I want to advance a conversation and get to the gospel so I can get to

13
00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:01.160
Jesus because I want them to know who Jesus is, I need a plan of attack.

14
00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:06.840
But I'm sure many of your students, maybe yourself, because I know I get there all

15
00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:12.300
the time, is what happens when they ask you a question that you can't answer?

16
00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:16.860
Because what happens is we get into these moments where we say, somebody asks us a

17
00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:19.560
question, we immediately want to have an answer to come right back and be like,

18
00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:21.460
yeah, I've got that answer and I've got that answer.

19
00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:26.000
Because we feel like if we have all of the answers, then we can somehow convince the

20
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:29.260
other person that what we believe is absolutely right because we've got all the

21
00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:32.820
answers to all their questions. Well, nothing could be further from the truth.

22
00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:37.780
That's not at all the case. And there are going to be times when we get stumped and

23
00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:42.220
we don't know what to say or the person that we're talking to has a belief system or

24
00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:44.680
a view on things that we're just not all that familiar with.

25
00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:47.780
You can't know everything about every single person.

26
00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:53.020
But there's a really simple strategy that I want you to sort of add in

27
00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:57.960
to talk with your students about, is when they're engaged in a conversation,

28
00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:02.020
you want to make sure that you are asking questions.

29
00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:06.840
And that's what this week is all about, the idea and the art of asking questions.

30
00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:12.960
Questions are so, so important. See, if we ask good questions,

31
00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:15.440
then we're going to get probably good answers.

32
00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:18.900
And just like any good teacher knows, you don't ask yes or no questions.

33
00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:24.860
You ask sort of in-depth, sort of probing questions, why kinds of questions to

34
00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:29.500
draw out of people the answers that you want or that you need.

35
00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:32.000
Well, the same goes true with our conversations.

36
00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:36.620
But this especially works if you have no idea what the other person is talking

37
00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:40.520
about. Because you can probe a little bit deeper and get them to talk through.

38
00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:46.320
At the same time, you're giving off the idea because you are

39
00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:50.700
and you want to be extremely interested in what they have to say.

40
00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:54.480
And that goes back to last week when we talked about love and building relationships

41
00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:59.900
and that sort of thing. So there are two specific questions that we can ask somebody

42
00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:04.420
if you get stumped. Okay, so the first question is simple.

43
00:03:07.210 --> 00:03:11.890
What do you mean?

44
00:03:12.910 --> 00:03:14.490
By that so

45
00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:21.840
this question is used when you're really not sure what all the person

46
00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:27.700
thinks person says something that you're just not all that clear on um I believe

47
00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:33.280
that Jesus is just a good moral teacher well what do you mean by that like you can

48
00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:37.800
probably guess what they might mean but maybe that's not what all the God just you

49
00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:41.660
know doesn't exist because there's too much evil in the world well what do you mean

50
00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:46.500
by that and if you're you can what do you mean by that people to death it's just you

51
00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:50.460
can go over and over and over again what do you mean by that or some variation of it

52
00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:54.560
but it's a very simple question it does two things one it digs deeper into the

53
00:03:54.560 --> 00:04:00.440
person what they're actually thinking but also buys you time to think about what you

54
00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:04.900
want to say a little bit so it allows them to clarify what they're saying so they

55
00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:08.980
can't just make a one-off statement to you, but it also buys you a little bit of

56
00:04:08.980 --> 00:04:14.120
time to think through their opinion more thoroughly and more thoughtfully.

57
00:04:14.440 --> 00:04:19.060
The second question is, how do you come to that conclusion?

58
00:04:19.420 --> 00:04:20.980
Or did you come?

59
00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:28.540
Again, people make one-off statements all the time.

60
00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:34.400
How'd they get there? So if they, again, they said something like, you know, God

61
00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:36.120
can't exist because there's too much evil in the world.

62
00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:41.160
Well how do you come to that conclusion there's a much larger explanation that I

63
00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:45.100
need for that and you don't have to you don't say it with kind of an attitude but

64
00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:48.800
you say it very like I'm interested like how did you come to that conclusion I

65
00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:53.260
really want to know I'm really interested in what you have to say what this does is

66
00:04:53.260 --> 00:04:57.880
this is helps them see that you are actually interested in what they have to say

67
00:04:57.880 --> 00:05:02.320
that you actually want to know what they think rather than just give them

68
00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:06.400
what you think. And there's a big difference between the two.

69
00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:09.460
And again, that's why last week and this week are connected together.

70
00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:13.480
Because by doing this kind of thing, you're showing them love.

71
00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:14.820
You're showing them relationships.

72
00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:17.280
You're showing them that you care about them as a person.

73
00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:19.400
Not just feeding them your opinion.

74
00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:26.180
The most valuable tool that you or your students have is the

75
00:05:26.180 --> 00:05:30.640
ability to sit down face-to-face with somebody to have a conversation.

76
00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:36.000
There's a lot of tips and tricks to sharing your faith, but the number one thing to

77
00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:41.940
keep in your toolbox is the ability to ask great questions and engage in deep,

78
00:05:42.040 --> 00:05:44.360
meaningful conversations with another person.

79
00:05:44.600 --> 00:05:48.540
It does so much more than just give the gospel.

80
00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:54.680
It builds relationships so that I'll say when that person finally receives

81
00:05:54.680 --> 00:06:00.680
Christ, that discipleship can actually begin rather

82
00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:01.760
than just a conversion.

